"Overlays repeated cycles on a common seasonal axis to compare their timing, shape, and magnitude.\n\nUse when: Several complete cycles have a meaningful shared seasonal position. Changes in peak timing or unusual individual cycles matter.\n\nAvoid when: Many cycles make paths indistinguishable or cycle lengths are incompatible.\n\nVisual grammar: Horizontal position encodes season within a cycle, such as month or hour. Vertical position encodes value; path identity encodes the cycle, such as year or day.\n\nBuild: Map timestamps to cycle identifiers and seasonal positions. Draw one chronologically ordered path per cycle on the same axes. Highlight only relevant cycles and put labels outside their endpoint positions.\n\nCheck: Check leap days and unequal cycle lengths follow an explicit policy. Verify values share the same units and temporal aggregation. Identify incomplete cycles with visible path endings.\n\nPitfalls: Crossings reveal cycles exchanging relative values and should not be artificially rerouted. Prefer seasonal subseries or separate panels when many paths become hard to follow.\n\nExample: Compare synthetic monthly sales profiles for six years, highlighting the latest year against muted earlier cycles.\n\nAlternatives: Seasonal subseries plot, Indexed line chart, Lasagna plot."
